#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"}] :question/id #uuid "bbd835f9-a428-558c-9db7-838d02267a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9512" :question/problem "Find a definite integral that's equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm{d} x$, where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,3)$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2109/Source/Images/4ab0e4cbe6e7788f713bbf2c1f85726e.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(3,3)$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= ⟨0 + t(3 - 0), 0 + t(3 - 0)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3t, 3t⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n0 \\le t \\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(3t) = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+y\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 3t + 3t \\\\\n&= 6t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)dx &= ∫_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\frac{dx}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}6t \\cdot 3dt \\\\\n&= 18∫_{0}^{1}tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133631 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"}] :question/id #uuid "7417f4d8-262c-5d16-9b35-f1c9710345e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9513" :question/problem "Find the definite integral equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_C x^2 \\, \\textrm d y$, where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $25x^2 + 4y^2 = 100$, traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,5)$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2109/Source/Images/59a1f8fa5056b041f2e3de53b64dc1d8.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 5\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nComputing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(5\\sin t) = 5\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (2\\cos t)^{2} = 4\\cos^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)dy &= ∫_{0}^{π/2}f(r(t))\\frac{dy}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}(4\\cos^{2} t) \\cdot 5\\cos tdt \\\\\n&= 20∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos^{3} tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133632 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "e45bef0c-86ba-5030-bb0c-274df22673f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9521" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_C \\left(x^2 + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm d y$, where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4$, traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,2)$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2109/Source/Images/b3d9fdb6a6ec0f267a2223a7f1436f91.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$. It can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nComputing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t) = 2\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= (2\\cos t)^{2} + (2\\sin t)^{2} \\\\\n&= 4(\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)f(x, y)dy &= ∫_{0}^{π/2}f(r(t))\\frac{dy}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π/2}4 \\cdot (2\\cos t) \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 8∫_{0}^{π/2}\\cos tdt \\\\\n&= 8\\sin t \\mid _{0}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= 8\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) - 8\\sin 0 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be40d020-6822-5029-925a-3124f2981988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} + 6t + 9)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130594} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3899e3-ef01-5379-82ae-d8264c0cd9b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} - 6t + 9)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130595} {:answer/id #uuid "1f959b34-fc55-50e2-a3ba-da60d9bcece8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} - 6t + 9)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130596} {:answer/id #uuid "25a2488d-b75d-5921-a2de-16466ae7fa61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6∫_{0}^{1}(9t^{2} - 6t + 1)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130597} {:answer/id #uuid "71db92a0-7728-5aaf-8417-e5e33c5c24dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12∫_{0}^{1}(9t^{2} - 6t + 1)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71db92a0-7728-5aaf-8417-e5e33c5c24dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12∫_{0}^{1}(9t^{2} - 6t + 1)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130598} :answer-field/id #uuid "a992ccb3-ead8-56f8-ad77-5cc490e0318b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130593}] :question/id #uuid "c9417c70-141e-5010-bf56-db9beb8a2e51" :question/math-academy-id "q-124121" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2109/Source/Images/q-124121.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $∫_{C}xydy$, where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-2)$ to the point $(2, 4)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = ⟨3t - 1, 6t - 2⟩$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c243e957-272a-5bde-8878-7264a94e447e" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f0cffb8-3fea-5129-b09a-745b3cef2711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10∫_{3π/2}^{2π}(5\\cos t - 4\\sin t)\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130606} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0d4148-aace-53d3-be61-ef1c36679bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{3π/2}^{2π}(5\\cos t - 4\\sin t)\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130607} {:answer/id #uuid "5b13b490-53c8-5f02-8676-4667617930da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{3π/2}^{2π}(5\\cos t - 4\\sin t)\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130608} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9dd6f2-467b-5f81-b8ee-67611c2a9442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5∫_{3π/2}^{2π}(5\\cos t - 4\\sin t)\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130609} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7fd6fb-5f02-541e-a6c4-440ef2d45908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10∫_{3π/2}^{2π}(5\\cos t - 4\\sin t)\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b9dd6f2-467b-5f81-b8ee-67611c2a9442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5∫_{3π/2}^{2π}(5\\cos t - 4\\sin t)\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130609} :answer-field/id #uuid "83a0d5ff-b5ed-54cb-8831-4fc545593f9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130605}] :question/id #uuid "f523c211-f380-5764-9fbd-9f50f1b0e80c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124230" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2109/Source/Images/q-124230.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $∫_{C}(x - y)dx$, where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16x^{2} + 25y^{2} = 400$, traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(5, 0)$, as shown above?\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = ⟨5\\cos t, 4\\sin t⟩$.*"}]}
